window.QUESTIONS.push(
Q(1, 'cloud', 'Consumption-based model',
  'A company is moving from buying servers to paying monthly for the compute and storage it actually uses. Which cloud model does this describe?',
  ['A consumption-based model', 'A capital expenditure model', 'A fixed-capacity model', 'A perpetual licensing model'],
  ['A'],
  'A consumption-based model bills for the resources used, such as compute hours and stored gigabytes, with no upfront hardware purchase. Costs become operating expenditure that rises and falls with demand.\n\n- **B** is wrong because capital expenditure is the upfront purchase of physical assets.\n- **C** is wrong because fixed capacity is the on-premises approach where you buy for peak demand.\n- **D** is wrong because perpetual licensing is a software licensing pattern, not a cloud pricing model.\n\n**Exam tip:** cloud shifts spend from CapEx to OpEx and from forecast capacity to actual usage.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the consumption-based model'),
Q(2, 'cloud', 'Cloud computing definition',
  'Which statement best defines cloud computing?',
  ['Delivery of computing services over the internet on a pay-as-you-go basis', 'Running all workloads on hardware owned by the customer', 'A licence that allows unlimited software installations', 'A private network that connects two datacenters'],
  ['A'],
  'Cloud computing is the delivery of compute, storage, networking, and other services over the internet, usually with consumption-based pricing and provider-managed infrastructure.\n\n- **B** describes on-premises computing.\n- **C** describes a software licence, not a delivery model.\n- **D** describes a network link such as ExpressRoute.\n\n**Exam tip:** the defining ideas are internet delivery, provider-managed hardware, and pay for what you use.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud computing'),
Q(3, 'cloud', 'Capital vs operating expenditure',
  'Which financial change is typical when a company migrates from on-premises servers to Azure?',
  ['Capital expenditure falls and operating expenditure rises', 'Both capital and operating expenditure fall', 'Capital expenditure rises and operating expenditure falls', 'Costs become fixed for the length of the contract'],
  ['A'],
  'Buying servers is capital expenditure: a large upfront asset purchase depreciated over time. Azure services are billed as operating expenditure, so the cost appears monthly and follows usage.\n\n- **B** is wrong because total spend may fall, but the shift to OpEx is the defining change.\n- **C** describes the reverse migration.\n- **D** is wrong because consumption pricing varies with use.\n\n**Exam tip:** CapEx buys assets; OpEx rents capacity.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the consumption-based model'),
Q(4, 'cloud', 'IaaS identification',
  'A team needs to run a custom application on Windows Server and must control the operating system, patches, and installed software. Which service model fits?',
  ['Infrastructure as a service', 'Platform as a service', 'Software as a service', 'Function as a service'],
  ['A'],
  'IaaS provides virtual machines and infrastructure while the customer manages the guest operating system, middleware, and applications. Azure Virtual Machines are the canonical example.\n\n- **B** is wrong because PaaS hides the operating system.\n- **C** is wrong because SaaS delivers a finished application.\n- **D** is a compute style, not one of the three cloud service models.\n\n**Exam tip:** if you patch the OS, you are in IaaS.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud service types'),
Q(5, 'cloud', 'PaaS identification',
  'Which cloud service model lets developers deploy code without managing the operating system or runtime?',
  ['Platform as a service', 'Infrastructure as a service', 'Software as a service', 'Desktop as a service'],
  ['A'],
  'PaaS supplies a managed platform for code: the provider runs the servers, operating system, runtime, and scaling, and the customer supplies the application and its data. Azure App Service and Azure SQL Database are examples.\n\n- **B** is wrong because IaaS leaves OS management with the customer.\n- **C** is wrong because SaaS is a finished application for end users.\n- **D** is wrong because desktop as a service delivers virtual desktops, not an app platform.\n\n**Exam tip:** PaaS is the middle ground - you bring code, Microsoft brings the platform.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud service types'),
Q(6, 'cloud', 'SaaS identification',
  'A company subscribes to Microsoft 365 and uses Exchange Online and Teams without installing or patching any servers. Which service model is this?',
  ['Software as a service', 'Platform as a service', 'Infrastructure as a service', 'Hybrid cloud'],
  ['A'],
  'SaaS delivers a complete application managed by the provider. Customers configure users, data, and settings, but do not manage the application, runtime, or servers.\n\n- **B** is wrong because PaaS gives a platform on which the customer deploys its own code.\n- **C** is wrong because IaaS gives virtual infrastructure the customer manages.\n- **D** is a deployment model, not a service model.\n\n**Exam tip:** Microsoft 365 is the standard SaaS example in AZ-900 questions.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud service types'),
Q(7, 'cloud', 'Choosing a service model',
  'A company wants to lift and shift an existing application to Azure with minimal changes and keep full control of the guest OS. Which model should it choose?',
  ['IaaS', 'PaaS', 'SaaS', 'Serverless'],
  ['A'],
  'Lift and shift moves the application as-is to virtual machines, which is IaaS. The customer keeps control of the OS and configuration, and the provider supplies the hardware and virtualization layer.\n\n- **B** would require refactoring or at least a supported runtime and removes OS control.\n- **C** would mean replacing the application with a vendor product.\n- **D** would require rewriting the code as event-driven functions.\n\n**Exam tip:** "minimal changes" plus "control of the OS" points to IaaS.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud service types'),
Q(8, 'cloud', 'Choosing a service model',
  'Developers want to publish a web app and an API without managing servers, and they want built-in scaling and deployment slots. Which Azure offering best fits?',
  ['Azure App Service', 'Azure Virtual Machines', 'Azure ExpressRoute', 'Azure Bastion'],
  ['A'],
  'App Service is a PaaS web host for apps, APIs, and background jobs. It provides managed scaling, deployment slots, TLS, and integration with source control, so the team ships code rather than servers.\n\n- **B** is wrong because VMs leave OS and patching work with the team.\n- **C** is a private network connection to Azure, not an app host.\n- **D** is a managed jump host for RDP and SSH.\n\n**Exam tip:** web apps plus no server management equals App Service.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe application hosting options'),
Q(9, 'cloud', 'Shared responsibility - always Microsoft',
  'Under the shared responsibility model, which responsibility always belongs to Microsoft, regardless of the service model?',
  ['The physical datacenter, hosts, and network', 'User accounts and their permissions', 'Data stored in the service', 'The configuration of the application'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft always owns the physical layer: datacenters, servers, storage hardware, and the network backbone. Which additional layers Microsoft manages depends on whether the service is IaaS, PaaS, or SaaS.\n\n- **B** is always the customer\'s responsibility, often shared with identity controls.\n- **C** is always the customer\'s data.\n- **D** is customer work for IaaS and PaaS and usually configuration for SaaS.\n\n**Exam tip:** the physical layer is Microsoft\'s in every model.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the shared responsibility model'),
Q(10, 'cloud', 'Shared responsibility - always customer',
  'Which responsibility always remains with the customer under the shared responsibility model?',
  ['Protecting data, accounts, and access', 'Maintaining the physical network', 'Patching the hypervisor', 'Replacing failed disks in a datacenter'],
  ['A'],
  'Data, identities, and access decisions stay with the customer in every model. The provider secures the platform, but it cannot decide who may read your data or which users should hold which permissions.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are provider responsibilities for cloud services: Microsoft operates the physical network, hypervisor, and hardware.\n\n**Exam tip:** "you always own your data and your identities" is a reliable rule.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the shared responsibility model'),
Q(11, 'cloud', 'Shared responsibility - IaaS',
  'In an IaaS deployment, which task remains the customer\'s responsibility?',
  ['Patching the guest operating system', 'Maintaining the physical hosts', 'Replacing failed network switches', 'Securing the datacenter building'],
  ['A'],
  'With IaaS, Microsoft manages the physical infrastructure and virtualization layer, while the customer manages the guest OS, middleware, applications, and data. Guest patching is therefore customer work.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are all physical-layer duties that belong to Microsoft.\n\n**Exam tip:** IaaS gives control, and control brings patching duty.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the shared responsibility model'),
Q(12, 'cloud', 'Shared responsibility - PaaS',
  'A team deploys code to Azure App Service. Which responsibility stays with the team?',
  ['The application code and its data', 'The operating system patches', 'The runtime and web server', 'The physical servers'],
  ['A'],
  'PaaS shifts OS, runtime, and server management to Microsoft, but the customer still owns the application code, its configuration, and the data it processes.\n\n- **B** and **C** are managed by the platform in App Service.\n- **D** is always Microsoft\'s responsibility.\n\n**Exam tip:** in PaaS, the dividing line sits just above the runtime.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the shared responsibility model'),
Q(13, 'cloud', 'Shared responsibility - SaaS',
  'For a SaaS application such as Microsoft 365, which task belongs to the customer?',
  ['Deciding which users can access which data', 'Patching the application servers', 'Maintaining the service runtime', 'Operating the datacenter'],
  ['A'],
  'SaaS leaves the customer responsible for its data, user accounts, and access configuration - for example, who may open a SharePoint site. Microsoft runs everything below that: application, runtime, OS, and hardware.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are provider duties in SaaS.\n\n**Exam tip:** in SaaS the customer configures, it does not operate.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the shared responsibility model'),
Q(14, 'cloud', 'Public cloud',
  'Which characteristic describes a public cloud deployment?',
  ['Resources are shared across customers and delivered over the internet by a provider', 'All hardware is dedicated to one organisation and hosted on its own site', 'Workloads run only on the customer\'s laptops', 'Two providers are used at the same time'],
  ['A'],
  'A public cloud is owned and operated by a provider and shared, with logical isolation, across many customers. Azure, Microsoft 365, and most Azure services are public cloud offerings.\n\n- **B** describes a private cloud.\n- **C** describes local computing.\n- **D** describes multi-cloud.\n\n**Exam tip:** "public" refers to provider ownership and multi-tenancy, not to whether data is public.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud deployment models'),
Q(15, 'cloud', 'Private cloud',
  'Which statement describes a private cloud?',
  ['Cloud services are used exclusively by one organisation', 'Services are sold to any customer over the internet', 'Workloads are split between two public providers', 'The service is free of charge'],
  ['A'],
  'A private cloud delivers cloud-style self-service and pooling but is dedicated to a single organisation. It can run in the organisation\'s datacenter or in a provider\'s dedicated environment.\n\n- **B** describes public cloud.\n- **C** describes multi-cloud.\n- **D** is unrelated to the deployment model.\n\n**Exam tip:** private means single-tenant, not necessarily on-premises.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud deployment models'),
Q(16, 'cloud', 'Hybrid cloud',
  'A hospital must keep patient records in its own datacenter by law but wants to run a new reporting portal in Azure. Which cloud model fits?',
  ['Hybrid cloud', 'Public cloud only', 'Private cloud only', 'Multi-cloud'],
  ['A'],
  'Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private infrastructure with public cloud, connected so that data and applications can move between them. Keeping records on site while hosting the portal in Azure is the classic hybrid pattern.\n\n- **B** would move the records off site and break the requirement.\n- **C** would not use Azure for the portal.\n- **D** means multiple public providers, which is not the issue here.\n\n**Exam tip:** regulatory data on site plus cloud services elsewhere equals hybrid.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud deployment models'),
Q(17, 'cloud', 'Multi-cloud',
  'A company uses Azure for analytics and a second provider for its website to avoid dependence on one vendor. Which term describes this?',
  ['Multi-cloud', 'Hybrid cloud', 'Private cloud', 'Public cloud'],
  ['A'],
  'Multi-cloud means using services from more than one cloud provider, often to reduce lock-in or to use a best-of-breed service. It differs from hybrid, which combines private infrastructure with public cloud.\n\n- **B** is about on-premises plus cloud.\n- **C** is a single-tenant cloud.\n- **D** is a single provider\'s shared cloud.\n\n**Exam tip:** multi-cloud counts providers; hybrid counts private plus public.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud deployment models'),
Q(18, 'cloud', 'Scalability',
  'What does scalability mean in cloud computing?',
  ['The ability to add or remove capacity to meet demand', 'The ability to recover from a regional outage', 'The ability to run without any servers', 'The ability to bill by the second'],
  ['A'],
  'Scalability is the ability to increase or decrease resources - more instances, larger sizes, or more storage - as workload demand changes.\n\n- **B** describes disaster recovery and resilience.\n- **C** describes serverless computing.\n- **D** describes a billing granularity.\n\n**Exam tip:** scalability is capacity change; elasticity is doing it automatically as demand moves.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(19, 'cloud', 'Elasticity',
  'A retail site automatically adds virtual machines during a sale and removes them afterwards. Which cloud benefit does this demonstrate?',
  ['Elasticity', 'Governance', 'High availability', 'Predictability'],
  ['A'],
  'Elasticity is automatic scaling that matches capacity to current demand, so the workload expands during peaks and contracts when they pass. The automation is what distinguishes it from manual scalability.\n\n- **B** is policy and compliance control.\n- **C** is about remaining available, not matching demand.\n- **D** is about consistent performance or cost forecasting.\n\n**Exam tip:** automatic scale out and back in is elasticity.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(20, 'cloud', 'Vertical scaling',
  'An administrator moves a database server from 4 vCPUs and 16 GB RAM to 16 vCPUs and 64 GB RAM. Which scaling approach is this?',
  ['Vertical scaling', 'Horizontal scaling', 'Elasticity', 'Load balancing'],
  ['A'],
  'Vertical scaling, or scaling up, increases the size of one resource - more CPU, memory, or storage on the same machine. Horizontal scaling adds more instances instead.\n\n- **B** would add servers rather than enlarge one.\n- **C** is automatic demand matching.\n- **D** distributes traffic across instances and does not change their size.\n\n**Exam tip:** up and down change size; out and in change count.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(21, 'cloud', 'Horizontal scaling',
  'A web tier adds three more identical virtual machines behind a load balancer. Which scaling approach is this?',
  ['Horizontal scaling', 'Vertical scaling', 'Scale up', 'Disaster recovery'],
  ['A'],
  'Horizontal scaling, or scaling out, adds instances so work is shared across them. It usually pairs with a load balancer and is the standard cloud pattern for stateless web tiers.\n\n- **B** and **C** mean making one machine larger.\n- **D** is recovery after a failure, not routine capacity growth.\n\n**Exam tip:** scale out equals more machines; scale up equals a bigger machine.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(22, 'cloud', 'High availability',
  'What does high availability aim to provide?',
  ['Minimal downtime through redundancy and fast recovery', 'The lowest possible monthly cost', 'A guarantee that data can never be lost', 'Automatic scaling during peak demand'],
  ['A'],
  'High availability designs out single points of failure - duplicated instances, zones, or regions - so the service stays reachable when a component fails. It reduces downtime but does not by itself prevent data loss.\n\n- **B** is cost optimisation.\n- **C** is durability and backup, a different goal.\n- **D** is elasticity.\n\n**Exam tip:** availability is about uptime; durability is about keeping data.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(23, 'cloud', 'Reliability',
  'What does reliability mean for a cloud workload?',
  ['The system keeps working correctly and recovers from failures', 'The system is always the cheapest option', 'The system stores data in every region', 'The system is available only during business hours'],
  ['A'],
  'Reliability is the ability of a system to continue operating correctly and to recover from failures. Cloud design achieves it through redundancy, health monitoring, and recovery from backups or replicas.\n\n- **B** is cost optimisation.\n- **C** is one possible durability technique, not the definition.\n- **D** describes a restricted service window.\n\n**Exam tip:** reliability is about behaving correctly through failure, not just staying online.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(24, 'cloud', 'Predictability',
  'A finance team needs consistent performance and a forecastable monthly bill. Which cloud benefit are they seeking?',
  ['Predictability', 'Agility', 'Elasticity', 'Governance'],
  ['A'],
  'Predictability covers both performance predictability, through services with defined capacity and SLAs, and cost predictability, through pricing tools, budgets, and consistent resource sizing.\n\n- **B** is speed of change.\n- **C** is automatic scaling.\n- **D** is policy and control.\n\n**Exam tip:** predictable performance and predictable cost are both part of this benefit.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(25, 'cloud', 'Agility',
  'A team can create a test environment in minutes instead of waiting weeks for hardware. Which cloud benefit does this show?',
  ['Agility', 'Durability', 'Governance', 'Elasticity'],
  ['A'],
  'Agility is the speed at which resources can be provisioned, changed, and released, which shortens experimentation and delivery cycles.\n\n- **B** is about protecting data over time.\n- **C** is about policy and compliance.\n- **D** is automatic demand matching, which is a scaling behaviour rather than provisioning speed.\n\n**Exam tip:** fast provisioning and fast teardown are agility.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(26, 'cloud', 'Economies of scale',
  'Why can cloud providers often deliver computing more cheaply per unit than a single company?',
  ['They buy and operate infrastructure at very large scale', 'They do not have to secure their datacenters', 'They pass all hardware costs to customers upfront', 'They only run workloads during business hours'],
  ['A'],
  'Economies of scale mean large providers purchase hardware, power, and networking in bulk and spread operational costs across many customers, lowering the cost per unit.\n\n- **B** is false - providers secure their facilities heavily.\n- **C** describes capital expenditure, which cloud avoids.\n- **D** is not how public cloud operates.\n\n**Exam tip:** scale lowers unit cost; that saving is what makes consumption pricing attractive.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(27, 'cloud', 'Serverless',
  'Which statement describes serverless computing?',
  ['Code runs on demand without the team provisioning or managing servers', 'Virtual machines are always running and billed hourly', 'The customer owns the physical servers', 'Applications must run inside a container cluster'],
  ['A'],
  'Serverless abstracts the infrastructure entirely: the platform allocates resources when an event occurs and the customer pays for execution. Azure Functions is the standard example.\n\n- **B** is traditional IaaS.\n- **C** is on-premises computing.\n- **D** describes container orchestration, which still involves managing nodes or a cluster.\n\n**Exam tip:** serverless means no server management and event-driven billing.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud service types'),
Q(28, 'cloud', 'Geo-distribution',
  'A company wants customers in Europe and Asia to reach its app with low latency. Which cloud capability should it use?',
  ['Deploy the app to multiple Azure regions', 'Move all data to one datacenter', 'Use a single virtual machine with a larger size', 'Disable caching at the edge'],
  ['A'],
  'Deploying to multiple regions puts compute and data closer to users, reducing network latency. Global routing services such as Front Door or Traffic Manager can then direct each user to the nearest healthy endpoint.\n\n- **B** increases distance for most users.\n- **C** adds capacity but not proximity.\n- **D** would usually make performance worse.\n\n**Exam tip:** latency problems are solved with geography, not bigger VMs.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(29, 'cloud', 'Disaster recovery',
  'Which pair of measures defines a disaster recovery plan?',
  ['Recovery time objective and recovery point objective', 'Return on investment and total cost of ownership', 'Service level agreement and acceptable use policy', 'Capital expenditure and operating expenditure'],
  ['A'],
  'Recovery time objective (RTO) is how long the service may be down; recovery point objective (RPO) is how much data loss is acceptable, measured in time. Together they drive replication and backup design.\n\n- **B** are financial measures.\n- **C** are contractual and policy documents.\n- **D** are accounting categories.\n\n**Exam tip:** RTO is downtime, RPO is data loss.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(30, 'cloud', 'Total cost of ownership',
  'What is the purpose of the Total Cost of Ownership calculator?',
  ['To compare the cost of an on-premises estate with running it in Azure', 'To estimate the cost of a single Azure virtual machine', 'To measure application response time', 'To compare Azure prices across regions only'],
  ['A'],
  'The TCO calculator compares on-premises costs - hardware, software, power, cooling, and labour - with the equivalent Azure services, so a migration business case shows the full picture.\n\n- **B** is the Pricing calculator, which prices Azure resources.\n- **C** is monitoring.\n- **D** is one input to pricing, not the calculator\'s purpose.\n\n**Exam tip:** TCO compares on-premises with Azure; Pricing calculator estimates an Azure bill.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(31, 'cloud', 'Cost drivers',
  'Which factors most directly affect the monthly cost of an Azure virtual machine? (Select two.)',
  ['The VM size and how long it runs', 'The colour of the resource group tag', 'The amount of outbound data transferred', 'The location assigned to the resource group'],
  ['A', 'C'],
  'Compute cost depends on the size (vCPU and memory) and the time the VM is running or allocated. Network egress, meaning data leaving Azure, is billed separately and can add significantly to a bill.\n\n- **B** has no cost effect; tags are metadata.\n- **D** is a metadata field: a resource group has a location for its own records, but pricing follows the region of each resource.\n\n**Exam tip:** compute time, storage volume, and outbound bandwidth are the three classic cost drivers.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the consumption-based model'),
Q(32, 'cloud', 'Choosing a deployment model',
  'A startup wants to avoid buying hardware, needs to scale quickly, and has no regulatory constraint. Which model is the best fit?',
  ['Public cloud', 'Private cloud', 'On-premises only', 'A single physical server'],
  ['A'],
  'Public cloud removes hardware purchase, provides elastic capacity, and needs no datacenter operations - exactly what a startup with no regulatory constraint needs.\n\n- **B** requires dedicated infrastructure and more operational effort.\n- **C** and **D** reinstate capital costs and limited scaling.\n\n**Exam tip:** default to public cloud unless a constraint forces otherwise.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud deployment models'),
Q(33, 'cloud', 'Hybrid use cases',
  'Which scenario is the strongest reason to choose a hybrid cloud model?',
  ['Some data must stay on-premises while new services run in the cloud', 'The company wants to use two public cloud providers', 'The company has no internet connection', 'The company wants to eliminate all datacenter costs immediately'],
  ['A'],
  'Hybrid keeps workloads where regulation, latency, or legacy dependencies require them while still using cloud services for everything else. Connectivity such as VPN Gateway or ExpressRoute links the two sides.\n\n- **B** is multi-cloud.\n- **C** would prevent cloud use.\n- **D** is a migration goal that hybrid may slow, not justify.\n\n**Exam tip:** hybrid is chosen for a constraint, not by default.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud deployment models'),
Q(34, 'cloud', 'Shared responsibility - identity',
  'Which identity task does the customer keep in every cloud service model?',
  ['Managing its own users, groups, and role assignments', 'Running the identity provider\'s servers', 'Patching the directory service', 'Operating the authentication datacenter'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft operates the identity platform, but the customer decides who exists in its tenant, which groups they belong to, and what they may access. Weak access control is a customer-side risk in IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS alike.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are provider duties for a managed identity service.\n\n**Exam tip:** the provider secures the platform; you secure your identities and data.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the shared responsibility model'),
Q(35, 'cloud', 'Cloud benefits',
  'Which of the following is a genuine benefit of cloud computing?',
  ['Rapid provisioning of resources with global reach', 'A guarantee that no security incident can occur', 'The removal of all responsibility from the customer', 'Fixed capacity purchased years in advance'],
  ['A'],
  'Cloud platforms provision resources in minutes and offer regions worldwide, which supports agility and geo-distribution. Customers still carry responsibilities, and security remains shared.\n\n- **B** is false; no provider guarantees zero incidents.\n- **C** is false; data, identities, and configuration stay with the customer.\n- **D** is the on-premises model.\n\n**Exam tip:** be wary of absolutes such as "all" or "never" in benefit questions.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(36, 'cloud', 'Service model comparison',
  'Which list orders the service models from most customer management to least?',
  ['IaaS, PaaS, SaaS', 'SaaS, PaaS, IaaS', 'PaaS, IaaS, SaaS', 'SaaS, IaaS, PaaS'],
  ['A'],
  'In IaaS the customer manages the OS and everything above it. In PaaS the provider manages the OS and runtime, leaving code and data. In SaaS the provider manages the whole application and the customer only configures it and supplies data.\n\n- **B** is the reverse order.\n- **C** and **D** misplace PaaS and IaaS.\n\n**Exam tip:** remember the stack: infrastructure, platform, software - control decreases left to right.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud service types'),
Q(37, 'cloud', 'Resilience concepts',
  'What is the difference between high availability and disaster recovery?',
  ['High availability avoids short outages; disaster recovery restores service after a major loss', 'They are the same thing', 'High availability restores data after deletion; disaster recovery prevents outages', 'Disaster recovery only applies to on-premises systems'],
  ['A'],
  'High availability uses redundancy to keep a service running through component failure, usually within a region. Disaster recovery plans how to restore service and data after a large event such as a region outage, often in another region.\n\n- **B** is false; they address different scales of failure.\n- **C** reverses the definitions.\n- **D** is false; Azure offers Site Recovery and backups for cloud workloads.\n\n**Exam tip:** HA is continuous uptime; DR is recovery after the big event.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(38, 'cloud', 'Consumption model',
  'Which statement about consumption-based pricing is correct?',
  ['You pay for resources while they are provisioned or used and stop paying when they are released', 'You pay a fixed annual fee regardless of use', 'You pay only for the physical servers you own', 'You pay the same amount every month by contract'],
  ['A'],
  'Consumption pricing meters usage - VM running time, storage held, requests, and egress. Deallocating or deleting resources stops the meter, which is why cost management matters in cloud workloads.\n\n- **B** and **D** describe fixed or reserved commitments, not pure consumption.\n- **C** describes owned hardware.\n\n**Exam tip:** if you forget to deallocate, you keep paying.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the consumption-based model'),
Q(39, 'cloud', 'Cloud benefits',
  'Which benefit is described as the ability to recover from failures and continue to function?',
  ['Reliability', 'Scalability', 'Agility', 'Manageability'],
  ['A'],
  'Reliability is the system\'s ability to keep functioning correctly and recover from failures. Cloud platforms support it with redundancy, health probes, and multi-region designs.\n\n- **B** is capacity adjustment.\n- **C** is speed of provisioning and change.\n- **D** is how easily the environment is operated and governed.\n\n**Exam tip:** reliability is about failure; availability is about uptime.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe the benefits of using cloud services'),
Q(40, 'cloud', 'Deployment model choice',
  'A government agency needs a cloud environment isolated from the public Azure instance for compliance. Which option should it consider?',
  ['Azure sovereign regions', 'A free Azure subscription', 'A multi-cloud deployment', 'Azure DevTest Labs'],
  ['A'],
  'Sovereign regions are isolated instances of Azure operated for specific compliance or legal requirements, such as US Government or China regions. They are physically and logically separated from the main Azure instance.\n\n- **B** is a billing offer, not an isolation boundary.\n- **C** uses multiple providers but not necessarily isolated infrastructure.\n- **D** is a lab environment service.\n\n**Exam tip:** sovereignty is about legal isolation, not pricing or tooling.',
  'Describe cloud concepts - Describe cloud deployment models')
,
Q(41, 'architecture', 'Azure regions',
  'What is an Azure region?',
  ['A geographic area containing one or more datacenters connected by a low-latency network', 'A single physical server rack', 'A billing account for one department', 'A container registry hosted by Microsoft'],
  ['A'],
  'A region is a geographic area with one or more datacenters networked together with low latency. When you deploy most resources you choose a region, which determines where data is stored and how close it is to users.\n\n- **B** is far smaller than a region.\n- **C** is a subscription or billing concept.\n- **D** is Azure Container Registry.\n\n**Exam tip:** region equals geography; availability zone equals datacenter isolation inside it.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(42, 'architecture', 'Region pairs',
  'Which two benefits are provided by Azure region pairs? (Select two.)',
  ['Planned updates roll out to paired regions one at a time', 'Data stays within the same geography for most pairs', 'Paired regions share a single datacenter', 'Region pairs remove the need for backups'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Region pairs are at least 300 miles apart within the same geography. Planned maintenance is sequenced across the pair to reduce downtime, and data residency is preserved because the pair stays in the same geography, with Brazil South as the notable exception.\n\n- **C** is false; the pair is deliberately separated.\n- **D** is false; replication and recovery still need configuration, and backups are separate.\n\n**Exam tip:** pairs give disaster resilience and update sequencing, not automatic backup.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(43, 'architecture', 'Availability zones',
  'What is an Azure availability zone?',
  ['A physically separate datacenter group within a region with independent power and cooling', 'A second subscription used for disaster recovery', 'A virtual network spread across countries', 'A pricing tier for storage accounts'],
  ['A'],
  'Availability zones are physically separate locations inside a region, each with independent power, cooling, and networking. Deploying across zones protects a workload from the loss of one datacenter.\n\n- **B** is a subscription, not a zone.\n- **C** describes a network design, not a zone.\n- **D** is a storage pricing tier.\n\n**Exam tip:** zones are inside a region; pairs are across regions.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(44, 'architecture', 'Availability zones',
  'How many availability zones does a region need at minimum to be zone-enabled?',
  ['Three', 'One', 'Two', 'Five'],
  ['A'],
  'Zone-enabled regions provide at least three availability zones so that a workload can survive the loss of one zone while maintaining quorum and capacity.\n\n- **B** and **C** do not provide the required isolation.\n- **D** is not the minimum; regions may have more than three in future.\n\n**Exam tip:** three zones is the standard AZ-900 answer.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(45, 'architecture', 'Global services',
  'Which Azure services are global rather than tied to a single region?',
  ['Microsoft Entra ID, Azure DNS, and Azure Traffic Manager', 'Azure Virtual Machines and managed disks', 'Azure Blob Storage and Azure Files', 'Azure App Service and Azure SQL Database'],
  ['A'],
  'Some services are global: Microsoft Entra ID, Azure DNS, and Traffic Manager do not require you to select a region when you create them. Most compute and storage resources are regional.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are deployed into a chosen region.\n\n**Exam tip:** identity, DNS, and global traffic routing are the classic global services.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(46, 'architecture', 'Resource groups',
  'Which statements about resource groups are true? (Select two.)',
  ['A resource belongs to exactly one resource group', 'Deleting a resource group deletes its resources', 'Resource groups can be nested inside each other', 'A resource group can span multiple subscriptions'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Every resource is in exactly one resource group. Actions at the group level apply to everything inside, and deleting the group deletes all its resources, which makes groups a useful lifecycle boundary.\n\n- **C** is false; groups cannot be nested.\n- **D** is false; a resource group belongs to one subscription.\n\n**Exam tip:** resource groups are flat, single-subscription containers.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(47, 'architecture', 'Subscriptions',
  'What are the two main boundaries provided by an Azure subscription?',
  ['Billing and access control', 'Networking and storage', 'Region and zone', 'Identity and encryption'],
  ['A'],
  'A subscription is a unit of management, billing, and scale. It produces separate invoices and applies access policies at its boundary, which is why organisations separate development and production by subscription.\n\n- **B** are resource types.\n- **C** are physical and resiliency scopes.\n- **D** are security features, not subscription boundaries.\n\n**Exam tip:** subscription equals invoice plus access boundary.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(48, 'architecture', 'Management groups',
  'Why would an organisation place several subscriptions under one management group?',
  ['To apply policy and access once and inherit it to every subscription', 'To share a single virtual network automatically', 'To reduce the number of regions available', 'To merge all subscriptions into one bill'],
  ['A'],
  'Management groups sit above subscriptions and let governance inherit downward. One policy assignment or RBAC assignment at the group applies to all child subscriptions, groups, and resources.\n\n- **B** is false; networking is not inherited.\n- **C** is unrelated.\n- **D** is false; each subscription keeps its own billing.\n\n**Exam tip:** management groups scale governance, not billing or networking.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(49, 'architecture', 'Management hierarchy',
  'Which order shows the Azure management hierarchy from highest to lowest?',
  ['Management groups, subscriptions, resource groups, resources', 'Subscriptions, management groups, resources, resource groups', 'Resources, resource groups, management groups, subscriptions', 'Resource groups, subscriptions, resources, management groups'],
  ['A'],
  'The tenant root group contains management groups, which contain subscriptions, which contain resource groups, which contain resources. Policy and access assignments inherit downward.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** place the levels in the wrong order.\n\n**Exam tip:** draw the chain once: tenant, management group, subscription, resource group, resource.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure', { diagram: 'resource-hierarchy' }),
Q(50, 'architecture', 'Management groups',
  'Which limit applies to management groups in a single Microsoft Entra directory?',
  ['10,000 management groups', '10 management groups', '100 subscriptions only', 'One management group per region'],
  ['A'],
  'A directory supports up to 10,000 management groups, and each management group or subscription can have only one parent. The hierarchy can be up to six levels deep below the root.\n\n- **B** is far too low.\n- **C** confuses subscriptions with the management group limit.\n- **D** is false; management groups are not regional.\n\n**Exam tip:** 10,000 groups, six levels, one parent.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(51, 'architecture', 'Resource organisation',
  'A team wants to delete a temporary dev environment in one action. What should it use?',
  ['Put all the resources in one resource group and delete the group', 'Delete each resource individually', 'Move the resources to another subscription', 'Create a management group for the environment'],
  ['A'],
  'Deleting a resource group deletes everything inside it, which makes groups the natural unit for temporary environments.\n\n- **B** is slower and error-prone.\n- **C** does not remove anything.\n- **D** adds governance scope but does not delete resources.\n\n**Exam tip:** group by lifecycle so teardown is one operation.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe core architectural components of Azure'),
Q(52, 'architecture', 'Virtual machines',
  'Which workload is the best fit for Azure Virtual Machines?',
  ['A legacy application that requires a specific OS configuration', 'A static website with no server-side code', 'A short event-driven task that runs in seconds', 'A managed relational database'],
  ['A'],
  'VMs suit lift-and-shift and custom OS requirements because the customer controls the operating system and installed software.\n\n- **B** is better on Blob static hosting or App Service.\n- **C** suits Azure Functions.\n- **D** suits Azure SQL Database.\n\n**Exam tip:** choose VMs when you need control, not when you want less administration.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(53, 'architecture', 'VM scale sets',
  'What is the main purpose of a virtual machine scale set?',
  ['Manage a group of identical load-balanced VMs that can scale automatically', 'Run containers without a cluster', 'Provide a managed relational database', 'Connect an on-premises network to Azure'],
  ['A'],
  'Scale sets centralise configuration and instance count, integrate with load balancing, and can scale out or in based on demand or a schedule.\n\n- **B** describes Azure Container Instances or Container Apps.\n- **C** describes Azure SQL.\n- **D** describes VPN Gateway or ExpressRoute.\n\n**Exam tip:** scale sets are for identical, stateless tiers.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(54, 'architecture', 'Availability sets',
  'How does an availability set improve resilience?',
  ['It spreads VMs across fault domains and update domains within a region', 'It replicates VMs to a second region automatically', 'It removes the need for load balancing', 'It stores VM data in multiple storage accounts'],
  ['A'],
  'An availability set groups VMs so they do not share a single power or network failure point (fault domain) and are not rebooted together during maintenance (update domain). This protects against host-level failures inside one datacenter.\n\n- **B** is region-pair or zone-level design.\n- **C** is unrelated.\n- **D** describes storage redundancy.\n\n**Exam tip:** availability sets protect within a datacenter; zones protect across datacenters.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(55, 'architecture', 'Containers',
  'Which statement about containers is correct?',
  ['They share the host operating system and start faster than virtual machines', 'Each container runs a full guest operating system', 'They cannot run in Azure', 'They require a physical server per container'],
  ['A'],
  'Containers package an application and its dependencies and share the host OS kernel, so they are lightweight and start quickly. VMs each run a full OS, which is heavier.\n\n- **B** describes VMs.\n- **C** is false; Azure offers Container Instances, Container Apps, and AKS.\n- **D** is false; many containers share a host.\n\n**Exam tip:** containers share the kernel; VMs do not.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(56, 'architecture', 'Azure Container Instances',
  'A team needs to run a single container for a short batch job with no orchestration. Which service is the simplest fit?',
  ['Azure Container Instances', 'Azure Kubernetes Service', 'Azure Virtual Machines', 'Azure App Service'],
  ['A'],
  'Container Instances run a container directly, billed by the second, with no cluster or VM management - ideal for short tasks and burst workloads.\n\n- **B** adds orchestration overhead that this job does not need.\n- **C** requires managing the host OS.\n- **D** is for web apps and APIs, not arbitrary batch containers.\n\n**Exam tip:** one container, quick start, no orchestration - ACI.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(57, 'architecture', 'Azure Kubernetes Service',
  'Which service is designed to orchestrate a large fleet of containers?',
  ['Azure Kubernetes Service', 'Azure Container Instances', 'Azure Functions', 'Azure Logic Apps'],
  ['A'],
  'AKS is a managed Kubernetes service that handles container scheduling, scaling, and lifecycle across a cluster of nodes.\n\n- **B** runs individual containers without orchestration.\n- **C** runs event-driven code, not container fleets.\n- **D** is a workflow automation service.\n\n**Exam tip:** many containers plus orchestration equals AKS.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(58, 'architecture', 'Azure Functions',
  'Which characteristic is true of Azure Functions?',
  ['It runs code in response to an event and scales automatically', 'It requires a dedicated VM to be always running', 'It can only run on Windows', 'It is a container registry'],
  ['A'],
  'Functions is event-driven serverless compute: a trigger such as an HTTP request, timer, or queue message starts the code, and the platform scales instances with demand. You pay for execution rather than idle capacity.\n\n- **B** contradicts serverless.\n- **C** is false; multiple languages and plans are supported.\n- **D** is Azure Container Registry.\n\n**Exam tip:** event plus code plus no server management equals Functions.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(59, 'architecture', 'Azure Functions',
  'What is a Durable Function used for?',
  ['Maintaining state across a long-running workflow', 'Storing blobs in a storage account', 'Balancing traffic across regions', 'Encrypting data at rest'],
  ['A'],
  'Durable Functions keep state and orchestrate multi-step workflows that outlast a single function execution, whereas ordinary functions are stateless by default.\n\n- **B** is Blob Storage.\n- **C** is Front Door or Traffic Manager.\n- **D** is storage encryption.\n\n**Exam tip:** stateful function workflows use Durable Functions.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(60, 'architecture', 'App Service',
  'Which statements about Azure App Service are true? (Select two.)',
  ['It hosts web apps and APIs without managing servers', 'It supports deployment from a Git repository', 'It gives the customer control of the guest operating system', 'It is a relational database service'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'App Service is a PaaS web host with built-in scaling, TLS, and continuous deployment from GitHub, Azure DevOps, or another Git repo.\n\n- **C** is false; the platform manages the OS.\n- **D** is false; Azure SQL Database is the relational database service.\n\n**Exam tip:** App Service is the default answer for web apps with minimal operations.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe application hosting options'),
Q(61, 'architecture', 'App Service',
  'A company needs a staging environment that can be swapped into production with no downtime. Which App Service feature should it use?',
  ['Deployment slots', 'Availability sets', 'Blob snapshots', 'Resource locks'],
  ['A'],
  'Deployment slots are live environments within an App Service app. Swapping a warmed staging slot into production redirects traffic with minimal interruption and allows quick rollback.\n\n- **B** is a VM feature.\n- **C** is storage.\n- **D** prevents changes rather than enabling safe release.\n\n**Exam tip:** staging plus swap equals deployment slots.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe application hosting options'),
Q(62, 'architecture', 'Azure Virtual Desktop',
  'What does Azure Virtual Desktop provide?',
  ['Managed Windows desktops and apps streamed to users from Azure', 'A container orchestration platform', 'A relational database service', 'A private network connection to Azure'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure Virtual Desktop centralises Windows desktop and app delivery in Azure, supports multi-session hosts, and integrates with Microsoft Entra ID for access control.\n\n- **B** is AKS.\n- **C** is Azure SQL.\n- **D** is VPN Gateway or ExpressRoute.\n\n**Exam tip:** VDI in Azure equals Azure Virtual Desktop.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(63, 'architecture', 'VM sizing',
  'Which VM family is optimised for memory-intensive workloads such as in-memory databases?',
  ['E-series', 'F-series', 'N-series', 'B-series'],
  ['A'],
  'E-series VMs provide a high memory-to-vCPU ratio for databases and analytics. F-series is compute optimised, N-series adds GPUs, and B-series is burstable for variable low-cost workloads.\n\n- **B** favours CPU.\n- **C** favours graphics and AI.\n- **D** favours intermittent use.\n\n**Exam tip:** match the letter to the workload: E memory, F compute, N GPU, B burst.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(64, 'architecture', 'Containers vs VMs',
  'Which two advantages do containers have over virtual machines? (Select two.)',
  ['Faster startup', 'Lower overhead because they share the host OS', 'A full independent operating system per instance', 'Stronger hardware isolation than a VM'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Containers start in seconds and use fewer resources because they share the host kernel and package only the app and dependencies.\n\n- **C** describes VMs, not containers.\n- **D** is false; VMs provide stronger isolation because each has its own OS.\n\n**Exam tip:** containers win on density and speed; VMs win on isolation and OS control.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(65, 'architecture', 'Compute choice',
  'A workload runs for a few seconds whenever a file is uploaded, and the company wants to pay only when it runs. Which compute option fits best?',
  ['Azure Functions', 'Azure Virtual Machines', 'Azure Kubernetes Service', 'Azure Virtual Desktop'],
  ['A'],
  'Functions is triggered by the upload event, runs the code, and bills for execution, with no cost while idle.\n\n- **B** bills while allocated, even when idle.\n- **C** requires cluster capacity.\n- **D** is a desktop service.\n\n**Exam tip:** short, event-driven, pay-per-run equals Functions.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure compute services'),
Q(66, 'architecture', 'Virtual networks',
  'What is the primary purpose of an Azure virtual network?',
  ['To let Azure resources communicate privately with each other, the internet, and on-premises networks', 'To store unstructured data', 'To host containers without a cluster', 'To provide a managed database'],
  ['A'],
  'A virtual network provides isolation and segmentation, routing, and connectivity between Azure resources and external networks. Subnets divide the address space and can host different tiers.\n\n- **B** is storage.\n- **C** is Container Instances.\n- **D** is Azure SQL.\n\n**Exam tip:** VNet equals private IP networking in Azure.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(67, 'architecture', 'Subnets',
  'Why are subnets used inside a virtual network?',
  ['To segment resources and apply network controls to each segment', 'To increase the number of public IP addresses', 'To replicate data across regions', 'To host management groups'],
  ['A'],
  'Subnets divide a VNet address space so tiers such as web, app, and data can be isolated and secured separately, for example with network security groups.\n\n- **B** is unrelated; public IPs are separate resources.\n- **C** is storage replication.\n- **D** is a governance scope, not a network concept.\n\n**Exam tip:** subnets plus NSGs provide network segmentation.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(68, 'architecture', 'VPN Gateway',
  'Which scenario calls for Azure VPN Gateway?',
  ['Connecting an on-premises network to a VNet over the public internet with encryption', 'Hosting a website without servers', 'Storing files in the cloud', 'Managing user identities'],
  ['A'],
  'VPN Gateway creates encrypted site-to-site, point-to-site, or VNet-to-VNet tunnels over the internet.\n\n- **B** is App Service or Functions.\n- **C** is Azure Storage.\n- **D** is Microsoft Entra ID.\n\n**Exam tip:** VPN equals encrypted tunnel over the internet.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(69, 'architecture', 'ExpressRoute',
  'A company requires a private, dedicated connection to Azure that does not traverse the public internet. Which service should it use?',
  ['Azure ExpressRoute', 'Azure VPN Gateway', 'Azure Front Door', 'Azure DNS'],
  ['A'],
  'ExpressRoute uses a connectivity provider to deliver a private circuit between on-premises networks and Azure, with predictable performance and no public internet path.\n\n- **B** uses encrypted tunnels over the internet.\n- **C** is global HTTP routing.\n- **D** is name resolution.\n\n**Exam tip:** dedicated private circuit equals ExpressRoute.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(70, 'architecture', 'Azure DNS',
  'What does Azure DNS provide?',
  ['Hosting and resolution of public DNS zones', 'A private tunnel to on-premises', 'A managed relational database', 'Container orchestration'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure DNS hosts public DNS zones and answers queries from Microsoft\'s global network, and private DNS zones resolve names inside virtual networks.\n\n- **B** is VPN or ExpressRoute.\n- **C** is Azure SQL.\n- **D** is AKS.\n\n**Exam tip:** DNS translates names; it does not route packets.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(71, 'architecture', 'Load Balancer',
  'At which layer does Azure Load Balancer distribute traffic?',
  ['Layer 4, using IP address and port', 'Layer 7, using URL paths', 'Layer 2, using MAC addresses only', 'Layer 3, using DNS names'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure Load Balancer is a layer 4 (TCP/UDP) service that distributes flows by IP address and port, with health probes removing failed instances.\n\n- **B** is Application Gateway or Front Door.\n- **C** and **D** are not how it operates.\n\n**Exam tip:** Load Balancer is fast and protocol-agnostic; layer 7 features need Application Gateway.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(72, 'architecture', 'Application Gateway',
  'Which feature distinguishes Azure Application Gateway from Azure Load Balancer?',
  ['URL path-based routing and TLS termination', 'Distribution at layer 4 only', 'Global anycast across continents', 'Storage of application logs'],
  ['A'],
  'Application Gateway is a layer 7 reverse proxy with path-based routing, TLS termination, cookie affinity, and optional WAF. Load Balancer works at layer 4.\n\n- **B** describes Load Balancer.\n- **C** describes Front Door.\n- **D** is Log Analytics.\n\n**Exam tip:** URL paths and web application firewall point to Application Gateway.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(73, 'architecture', 'Front Door',
  'Which service provides global HTTP load balancing with edge caching and WAF?',
  ['Azure Front Door', 'Azure Application Gateway', 'Azure Load Balancer', 'Azure VPN Gateway'],
  ['A'],
  'Front Door is a global anycast entry point for HTTP(S) traffic, with latency-based routing, failover, caching, and web application firewall at the edge.\n\n- **B** is regional layer 7.\n- **C** is regional layer 4.\n- **D** is a VPN.\n\n**Exam tip:** global web acceleration and failover equals Front Door.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services', { diagram: 'networking-path' }),
Q(74, 'architecture', 'Network security groups',
  'What does a network security group do?',
  ['Filters inbound and outbound traffic by rules', 'Encrypts data at rest', 'Replicates storage across regions', 'Manages user sign-ins'],
  ['A'],
  'An NSG contains allow and deny rules evaluated by priority, and can be associated with a subnet or a network interface to control traffic.\n\n- **B** is storage encryption.\n- **C** is storage replication.\n- **D** is Entra ID.\n\n**Exam tip:** NSG equals traffic filtering, not encryption.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(75, 'architecture', 'Connectivity choice',
  'A bank needs predictable latency and a private connection from its datacenter to Azure. Which connectivity option should it choose?',
  ['ExpressRoute', 'Site-to-site VPN', 'Public internet only', 'Azure Bastion'],
  ['A'],
  'ExpressRoute provides a dedicated private circuit with predictable performance, which suits strict latency and compliance needs.\n\n- **B** is encrypted but still traverses the public internet.\n- **C** offers neither privacy nor predictability.\n- **D** is a jump host for management access.\n\n**Exam tip:** private plus predictable equals ExpressRoute.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure networking services'),
Q(76, 'architecture', 'Storage services',
  'Which Azure Storage service should hold millions of product photographs?',
  ['Blob storage', 'Queue storage', 'Table storage', 'Azure Files'],
  ['A'],
  'Blob storage is optimised for large amounts of unstructured data such as images, video, backups, and documents, and supports hot, cool, cold, and archive tiers.\n\n- **B** holds messages.\n- **C** holds NoSQL key-value data.\n- **D** provides SMB file shares.\n\n**Exam tip:** unstructured objects equal blobs.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services', { diagram: 'storage-services' }),
Q(77, 'architecture', 'Blob access tiers',
  'A blob is accessed rarely but must be kept for years at the lowest storage cost. Which tier fits best?',
  ['Archive', 'Hot', 'Cool', 'Premium'],
  ['A'],
  'The archive tier is the cheapest for storage but is offline: rehydration is required before reading, and early deletion charges apply before 180 days.\n\n- **B** is for frequent access.\n- **C** is for infrequent access with 30-day minimum.\n- **D** is a performance tier for block blobs, not an access tier.\n\n**Exam tip:** cheap and rarely read equals archive, but plan for rehydration delay.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(78, 'architecture', 'Azure Files',
  'Which access method is supported by Azure Files?',
  ['SMB and NFS file shares', 'AMQP message queues', 'SQL queries only', 'Container image pulls'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure Files provides managed file shares over SMB (and NFS on premium), which can be mounted by Windows, Linux, and macOS clients or accessed from code.\n\n- **B** is Queue storage.\n- **C** is Azure SQL.\n- **D** is Azure Container Registry.\n\n**Exam tip:** lift-and-shift file shares use Azure Files.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(79, 'architecture', 'Queue storage',
  'What is Azure Queue storage used for?',
  ['Storing messages between application components', 'Serving web pages', 'Holding relational tables', 'Hosting virtual machines'],
  ['A'],
  'Queue storage holds messages that decouple producers from consumers, so components can process work asynchronously and absorb spikes.\n\n- **B** is App Service or Storage static hosting.\n- **C** is Table storage or Azure SQL.\n- **D** is compute.\n\n**Exam tip:** decoupling components points to a queue.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(80, 'architecture', 'Table storage',
  'Which kind of data model does Azure Table storage use?',
  ['A NoSQL key-value store with a schema-less design', 'A relational model with foreign keys', 'A graph of nodes and edges', 'A columnar data warehouse'],
  ['A'],
  'Table storage is a NoSQL key-value store for structured, non-relational data. It scales cheaply and suits large volumes that do not need joins.\n\n- **B** describes Azure SQL Database.\n- **C** describes a graph database.\n- **D** describes Synapse or another analytics store.\n\n**Exam tip:** key-value plus no joins equals Table storage.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(81, 'architecture', 'Storage redundancy',
  'What does locally redundant storage (LRS) provide?',
  ['Three copies within a single datacenter', 'Three copies across three availability zones', 'Six copies across two regions', 'One copy in a single disk'],
  ['A'],
  'LRS keeps three copies of the data within one datacenter in the primary region, protecting against disk and rack failure but not zone or region loss.\n\n- **B** is ZRS.\n- **C** is GRS or RA-GRS.\n- **D** is not a redundancy option.\n\n**Exam tip:** LRS is local and cheapest; it does not survive a datacenter loss.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(82, 'architecture', 'Storage redundancy',
  'A storage account must remain readable from a secondary region if the primary region fails. Which option should be used?',
  ['Read-access geo-redundant storage (RA-GRS)', 'Locally redundant storage (LRS)', 'Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)', 'Standard HDD'],
  ['A'],
  'RA-GRS replicates to a paired region and exposes a read-only secondary endpoint, so reads can continue during a primary outage. GRS replicates but does not allow reads until failover.\n\n- **B** is single-region.\n- **C** is multi-zone but single-region.\n- **D** is a disk type, not a replication option.\n\n**Exam tip:** readable secondary equals RA-GRS or RA-GZRS.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(83, 'architecture', 'Storage redundancy',
  'Which redundancy option copies data across three availability zones in one region?',
  ['Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)', 'Locally redundant storage (LRS)', 'Geo-redundant storage (GRS)', 'Read-access geo-redundant storage (RA-GRS)'],
  ['A'],
  'ZRS spreads three copies across three zones in the primary region, so the data survives the loss of one zone without leaving the region.\n\n- **B** keeps copies in one datacenter.\n- **C** and **D** replicate to a secondary region.\n\n**Exam tip:** zones equal ZRS; regions equal GRS.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(84, 'architecture', 'Storage account naming',
  'Which name is valid for an Azure storage account?',
  ['contosostore2026', 'Contoso-Store', 'contoso_store', 'contoso.store'],
  ['A'],
  'Storage account names must be 3 to 24 characters, use lowercase letters and numbers only, and be globally unique across Azure. The name forms part of the service endpoint.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** contain uppercase letters, hyphens, underscores, or periods.\n\n**Exam tip:** lowercase and numbers only, 3 to 24 characters.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(85, 'architecture', 'Data migration',
  'A company must move 80 TB of files to Azure once, over a slow internet link. Which service is designed for this?',
  ['Azure Data Box', 'AzCopy over the internet', 'Azure Storage Explorer', 'Azure Files Sync'],
  ['A'],
  'Data Box ships a physical appliance to the site; data is copied locally and the device is returned to Microsoft, avoiding a long transfer over a constrained link.\n\n- **B** and **C** are software tools that still use the network.\n- **D** synchronises file shares but does not solve a one-off 80 TB transfer over a slow link.\n\n**Exam tip:** too much data for the link equals Data Box.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(86, 'architecture', 'Storage durability',
  'What does data durability mean for a storage service?',
  ['Data remains intact and available over time, even when hardware fails', 'Data can be read from any region at any time', 'Data is encrypted with customer keys', 'Data is compressed automatically'],
  ['A'],
  'Durability is about keeping data intact over time through replication and error detection. Availability is about being able to reach it, and redundancy supports both but is not the definition.\n\n- **B** describes read access, not durability.\n- **C** is encryption.\n- **D** is a performance feature.\n\n**Exam tip:** durability protects the data; availability protects access.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services'),
Q(87, 'architecture', 'Microsoft Entra ID',
  'What is the primary role of Microsoft Entra ID?',
  ['Cloud identity and access management for users, groups, and applications', 'Storing files in the cloud', 'Hosting virtual machines', 'Filtering network traffic'],
  ['A'],
  'Entra ID is the cloud directory and identity provider: it authenticates users and apps, manages groups, and issues tokens used to authorise access to Azure and Microsoft 365.\n\n- **B** is storage.\n- **C** is compute.\n- **D** is an NSG or firewall.\n\n**Exam tip:** identity equals Entra ID; authorisation to Azure resources then uses RBAC.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(88, 'architecture', 'Authentication vs authorization',
  'What is the difference between authentication and authorization?',
  ['Authentication proves identity; authorization decides what that identity may do', 'Authorization proves identity; authentication grants permissions', 'They are the same process', 'Authentication applies only to applications'],
  ['A'],
  'Authentication verifies who or what is connecting, for example with a password, certificate, or token. Authorization then evaluates permissions, such as an RBAC role assignment, to decide which actions are allowed.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is false.\n- **D** is false; users and apps both authenticate.\n\n**Exam tip:** authenticate first, authorize second.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(89, 'architecture', 'Multi-factor authentication',
  'Which two factors can combine to satisfy multi-factor authentication? (Select two.)',
  ['A password', 'A code from an authenticator app', 'A username', 'An email address'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'MFA requires two or more different factor types: something you know (password), something you have (phone or authenticator app), or something you are (biometrics).\n\n- **C** and **D** are identifiers, not authentication factors, and both are "something you know" in any case.\n\n**Exam tip:** two factors must be from different categories.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(90, 'architecture', 'Conditional Access',
  'What does Microsoft Entra Conditional Access evaluate?',
  ['Signals such as user, device, location, and app to decide whether to allow access', 'The cost of Azure resources', 'Network packet size', 'Storage redundancy settings'],
  ['A'],
  'Conditional Access applies if-then policies using signals from the sign-in. For example, it can require MFA when a user signs in from an unfamiliar location or block access from unmanaged devices.\n\n- **B** is cost management.\n- **C** is networking.\n- **D** is storage configuration.\n\n**Exam tip:** Conditional Access is policy-based sign-in control.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(91, 'architecture', 'Single sign-on',
  'What benefit does single sign-on provide?',
  ['One authentication lets a user access multiple applications', 'One password is shared by every user', 'All applications bypass MFA', 'Users no longer need accounts'],
  ['A'],
  'SSO uses a central identity provider so a user signs in once and receives tokens for many applications, reducing password fatigue and sign-in friction without removing individual accounts.\n\n- **B** is insecure and unrelated.\n- **C** is false; policies can still require MFA.\n- **D** is false.\n\n**Exam tip:** SSO is convenience plus central control, not fewer identities.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(92, 'architecture', 'External identities',
  'A company wants partners to access its Teams channels using their own work accounts. Which Entra ID capability supports this?',
  ['B2B collaboration', 'B2C identity', 'Managed identities', 'Conditional Access only'],
  ['A'],
  'B2B collaboration invites external users as guests, and they authenticate with their home organisation credentials. B2C is for consumer-facing applications, and managed identities are for Azure workloads.\n\n- **B** targets customers, not partners.\n- **C** is for services, not people.\n- **D** is a policy engine, not an invitation mechanism.\n\n**Exam tip:** partners equals B2B guests; customers equals B2C.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(93, 'architecture', 'Azure AI services',
  'A developer wants to add vision and speech features to an app without training a model. Which service category fits?',
  ['Azure AI services', 'Azure Machine Learning', 'Azure IoT Hub', 'Azure Synapse Analytics'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure AI services provide prebuilt APIs for vision, speech, language, and document processing, so teams add intelligence without model development.\n\n- **B** is for building and training custom models.\n- **C** connects IoT devices.\n- **D** is analytics.\n\n**Exam tip:** prebuilt AI APIs equal Azure AI services; custom models equal Azure Machine Learning.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure AI services'),
Q(94, 'architecture', 'IoT services',
  'Which service provides secure bidirectional communication between cloud applications and IoT devices?',
  ['Azure IoT Hub', 'Azure IoT Central', 'Azure Functions', 'Azure Front Door'],
  ['A'],
  'IoT Hub is the managed message hub for device-to-cloud and cloud-to-device communication, with device identity and monitoring. IoT Central is a SaaS layer built on top of IoT capabilities for solution builders.\n\n- **B** is a simplified SaaS IoT platform rather than the core hub.\n- **C** is compute.\n- **D** is web traffic routing.\n\n**Exam tip:** device telemetry at scale equals IoT Hub.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure IoT services'),
Q(95, 'architecture', 'Choosing a service',
  'A company wants a managed relational database for an order system without patching a database server. Which service should it choose?',
  ['Azure SQL Database', 'Azure Table storage', 'Azure Blob storage', 'Azure Queue storage'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure SQL Database is a managed relational database service: Microsoft handles patching, backups, and high availability while the customer designs schemas and queries.\n\n- **B** is NoSQL key-value storage without relational joins.\n- **C** is object storage.\n- **D** is messaging.\n\n**Exam tip:** relational plus managed equals Azure SQL Database.',
  'Describe Azure architecture and services - Describe Azure storage services')
,
Q(96, 'governance', 'Pricing calculator',
  'A team wants to estimate the monthly cost of a planned set of Azure resources before deploying them. Which tool should it use?',
  ['Azure Pricing calculator', 'Total Cost of Ownership calculator', 'Azure Advisor', 'Azure Monitor'],
  ['A'],
  'The Pricing calculator lets you add the specific Azure services, sizes, and quantities you plan to use and produces an estimated monthly bill.\n\n- **B** compares an existing on-premises estate with Azure, rather than pricing a new design.\n- **C** gives recommendations on deployed resources.\n- **D** monitors health and performance.\n\n**Exam tip:** new design equals Pricing calculator; migration business case equals TCO calculator.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe cost management'),
Q(97, 'governance', 'Cost analysis',
  'Which tool shows actual spend broken down by resource, resource group, or tag?',
  ['Azure Cost analysis', 'Azure Service Health', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud', 'Azure Policy'],
  ['A'],
  'Cost analysis in Microsoft Cost Management visualises accumulated costs and lets you group and filter by subscription, resource group, service, or tag, which is how chargeback and trend reviews are done.\n\n- **B** reports platform incidents and maintenance.\n- **C** reports security posture.\n- **D** enforces configuration rules.\n\n**Exam tip:** actual spend and trends equals Cost analysis.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe cost management'),
Q(98, 'governance', 'Budgets',
  'What does an Azure budget do?',
  ['Tracks spend against a threshold and can trigger alerts or actions', 'Blocks the creation of expensive resources', 'Encrypts billing data', 'Provides a discount for prepayment'],
  ['A'],
  'Budgets set a spending target for a scope such as a subscription or resource group and send alerts when actual or forecast cost crosses configured thresholds. They do not stop spending by themselves.\n\n- **B** is a policy or quota control, not a budget.\n- **C** is unrelated.\n- **D** describes reservations or savings plans.\n\n**Exam tip:** budgets warn; they do not enforce.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe cost management'),
Q(99, 'governance', 'Tags',
  'How can tags help with cost management?',
  ['They group costs by business dimension such as department or project', 'They lower the price of a resource automatically', 'They encrypt the resource', 'They increase the resource quota'],
  ['A'],
  'Tags are name-value metadata. When applied consistently, they let Cost analysis group spend by cost centre, environment, or owner, which supports chargeback.\n\n- **B** is false; tags do not change price.\n- **C** is encryption.\n- **D** is a quota matter.\n\n**Exam tip:** tags organise and report; they do not enforce or discount.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe cost management'),
Q(100, 'governance', 'Cost factors',
  'Which two factors affect the cost of an Azure solution? (Select two.)',
  ['The region where resources are deployed', 'The amount of outbound data transfer', 'The number of tags on a resource', 'The name of the resource group'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Prices vary by region because power, land, and labour costs differ, and network egress is billed separately from compute and storage.\n\n- **C** is metadata with no price effect.\n- **D** is a naming choice.\n\n**Exam tip:** region, resource size, running time, storage, and egress drive most bills.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe cost management'),
Q(101, 'governance', 'Cost recommendations',
  'Which service recommends resizing or shutting down underused virtual machines to reduce cost?',
  ['Azure Advisor', 'Azure Policy', 'Azure Monitor', 'Microsoft Entra ID'],
  ['A'],
  'Advisor analyses resource configuration and usage and offers recommendations across reliability, security, performance, cost, and operational excellence - including right-sizing idle or underused VMs.\n\n- **B** enforces rules.\n- **C** observes telemetry.\n- **D** manages identity.\n\n**Exam tip:** free, personalised recommendations equal Advisor.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe cost management'),
Q(102, 'governance', 'Role-based access control',
  'What does Azure RBAC control?',
  ['Who can perform which actions on which Azure resources', 'How much a resource costs', 'How data is replicated', 'Which region a resource uses'],
  ['A'],
  'RBAC grants access by assigning a role definition to a security principal at a scope. The role lists allowed actions, and the scope decides where the assignment applies.\n\n- **B** is cost management.\n- **C** is storage redundancy.\n- **D** is a deployment choice.\n\n**Exam tip:** RBAC answers "who can do what, where".',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(103, 'governance', 'RBAC scopes',
  'At which scopes can an Azure RBAC role be assigned?',
  ['Management group, subscription, resource group, and resource', 'Only the subscription', 'Only the resource group', 'Only individual resources'],
  ['A'],
  'RBAC assignments can target a management group, subscription, resource group, or individual resource, and permissions inherit to child scopes.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are each too restrictive; all four levels are valid.\n\n**Exam tip:** assign at the highest scope that still meets least privilege.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(104, 'governance', 'Built-in roles',
  'Which built-in role can manage all resources but cannot grant access to others?',
  ['Contributor', 'Owner', 'Reader', 'User Access Administrator'],
  ['A'],
  'Contributor can create and manage resources but cannot assign roles. Owner has full control including access management, Reader can only view, and User Access Administrator manages access only.\n\n- **B** can grant access.\n- **C** cannot change resources.\n- **D** manages access but not resources.\n\n**Exam tip:** Contributor equals manage without delegation.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(105, 'governance', 'Least privilege',
  'What does the principle of least privilege require?',
  ['Grant only the permissions needed for the task, for the shortest necessary time', 'Grant every user the Owner role for flexibility', 'Remove all roles from administrators', 'Share one account across the team'],
  ['A'],
  'Least privilege limits the blast radius of mistakes and compromised accounts by granting the minimum permissions at the narrowest scope for the time required.\n\n- **B** is the opposite.\n- **C** would prevent administration.\n- **D** removes accountability.\n\n**Exam tip:** least privilege is about scope, role, and duration.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(106, 'governance', 'Azure Policy',
  'What is Azure Policy used for?',
  ['Enforcing rules about resource configuration, such as allowed regions or required tags', 'Assigning roles to users', 'Monitoring application performance', 'Replicating storage'],
  ['A'],
  'Policy evaluates resource properties against definitions and can audit, deny, or remediate non-compliant resources. It controls what a resource may look like, not who can create it.\n\n- **B** is RBAC.\n- **C** is Azure Monitor.\n- **D** is storage redundancy.\n\n**Exam tip:** Policy governs configuration; RBAC governs people.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(107, 'governance', 'Policy vs RBAC',
  'A company wants to prevent anyone from creating resources outside West Europe, even subscription owners. Which control should it use?',
  ['Azure Policy with a deny effect', 'An RBAC Reader role', 'A resource lock', 'A budget alert'],
  ['A'],
  'Policy deny assignments block non-compliant create and update requests, and they apply regardless of the user\'s RBAC role, including Owner.\n\n- **B** only restricts actions, not configuration values.\n- **C** prevents deletion or changes to existing resources, not region choice.\n- **D** reports cost.\n\n**Exam tip:** to constrain what can be deployed, use Policy.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(108, 'governance', 'Resource locks',
  'Which lock prevents a resource from being deleted while still allowing changes?',
  ['CanNotDelete', 'ReadOnly', 'Deny', 'Audit'],
  ['A'],
  'CanNotDelete blocks deletion but permits read and write operations. ReadOnly blocks both changes and deletion.\n\n- **B** blocks modifications as well as deletion.\n- **C** and **D** are Policy effects, not lock types.\n\n**Exam tip:** CanNotDelete equals no deletion; ReadOnly equals no changes.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(109, 'governance', 'Resource locks',
  'What does a ReadOnly lock do?',
  ['Prevents changes and deletion of the resource', 'Prevents deletion only', 'Allows changes but blocks reads', 'Encrypts the resource'],
  ['A'],
  'ReadOnly locks make a resource behave as read-only: updates and deletes are blocked. The lock is inherited from its parent scope and must be removed before changes are possible.\n\n- **B** is CanNotDelete.\n- **C** is not a valid behaviour.\n- **D** is encryption.\n\n**Exam tip:** locks are a blunt guard rail; they apply to everyone, including Owners.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(110, 'governance', 'Tags',
  'Which statements about tags are true? (Select two.)',
  ['Tags are name-value pairs used for organisation and reporting', 'Tags are not inherited from a resource group automatically', 'Tags block resource deletion', 'Tags are encrypted secrets'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Tags describe resources - for example Environment equals Production - and support cost reporting and automation. A resource does not automatically receive its resource group\'s tags.\n\n- **C** is a lock.\n- **D** is Key Vault.\n\n**Exam tip:** apply tags with Policy or automation if you need consistency.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(111, 'governance', 'Governance at scale',
  'A company has 40 subscriptions and wants one set of compliance rules applied everywhere. What should it use?',
  ['Assign Azure Policy at a management group', 'Assign Reader at each resource', 'Apply a lock to each resource group', 'Create a budget in each subscription'],
  ['A'],
  'Management groups let one policy assignment inherit to every child subscription and resource, which is the scalable way to enforce a baseline.\n\n- **B** is access, not configuration compliance, and does not scale well.\n- **C** guards deletion, not configuration.\n- **D** reports cost.\n\n**Exam tip:** governance at scale starts at the management group.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(112, 'governance', 'Azure Monitor',
  'What is the purpose of Azure Monitor?',
  ['Collecting and analysing telemetry such as metrics and logs', 'Assigning RBAC roles', 'Deploying ARM templates', 'Managing DNS records'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure Monitor collects platform and guest telemetry, stores metrics and logs, and powers alerts, dashboards, and workbooks.\n\n- **B** is Entra ID and RBAC.\n- **C** is the deployment toolchain.\n- **D** is Azure DNS.\n\n**Exam tip:** Monitor observes; it does not configure or deploy.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(113, 'governance', 'Metrics and logs',
  'Which statement describes the difference between metrics and logs in Azure Monitor?',
  ['Metrics are numeric time-series values; logs are detailed records queried with KQL', 'Metrics are text records; logs are numeric values', 'Metrics are stored only on-premises', 'Logs cannot be queried'],
  ['A'],
  'Metrics are lightweight numeric samples collected at intervals, ideal for charts and threshold alerts. Logs are richer event records stored in a workspace and queried with Kusto Query Language.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is false.\n- **D** is false; Log Analytics queries logs.\n\n**Exam tip:** numbers over time equal metrics; searchable events equal logs.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(114, 'governance', 'Log Analytics',
  'Which language is used to query data in a Log Analytics workspace?',
  ['Kusto Query Language (KQL)', 'T-SQL only', 'PowerShell only', 'YAML'],
  ['A'],
  'Log Analytics uses KQL, a read-only query language with operators such as where, summarize, and project for filtering, aggregating, and shaping results.\n\n- **B** is for relational databases.\n- **C** is a scripting shell, not the query language.\n- **D** is configuration markup.\n\n**Exam tip:** Log Analytics equals KQL.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(115, 'governance', 'Alerts',
  'An administrator wants an email whenever CPU on a VM exceeds 90 percent. What should be configured?',
  ['An alert rule with an action group', 'A resource lock', 'A budget', 'A DNS record'],
  ['A'],
  'An alert rule defines the signal and threshold - here the CPU metric - and an action group defines who is notified or which automation runs.\n\n- **B** prevents changes.\n- **C** tracks cost.\n- **D** resolves names.\n\n**Exam tip:** alert rule detects; action group responds.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(116, 'governance', 'Service Health',
  'Which service tells an administrator about Azure platform outages and planned maintenance affecting their subscriptions?',
  ['Azure Service Health', 'Azure Advisor', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud', 'Azure Cost analysis'],
  ['A'],
  'Service Health reports current incidents, planned maintenance, health advisories, and security advisories scoped to the services and regions the subscription uses.\n\n- **B** gives best-practice recommendations.\n- **C** reports security posture.\n- **D** reports cost.\n\n**Exam tip:** platform incidents equal Service Health; your own resources equal Azure Monitor.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(117, 'governance', 'Advisor',
  'Which five categories does Azure Advisor make recommendations for?',
  ['Reliability, security, performance, cost, and operational excellence', 'Identity, storage, compute, network, and backup', 'Development, test, staging, production, and archive', 'Bronze, silver, gold, platinum, and premier'],
  ['A'],
  'Advisor reviews deployed resources and recommends improvements in reliability, security, performance, cost, and operational excellence. It is free for every subscription.\n\n- **B** lists resource domains, not Advisor categories.\n- **C** and **D** are unrelated classifications.\n\n**Exam tip:** remember the five Advisor pillars.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(118, 'governance', 'Defender for Cloud',
  'What is Microsoft Defender for Cloud?',
  ['A cloud security posture management and workload protection service', 'A cost reporting tool', 'A DNS hosting service', 'A virtual machine size family'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Cloud assesses configuration against security benchmarks, assigns a secure score, and provides threat protection for workloads. It works across Azure, other clouds, and on-premises.\n\n- **B** is Cost Management.\n- **C** is Azure DNS.\n- **D** is compute sizing.\n\n**Exam tip:** security posture and secure score equal Defender for Cloud.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(119, 'governance', 'Secure score',
  'What does the secure score in Microsoft Defender for Cloud measure?',
  ['How well the environment follows security best practices', 'The monthly Azure bill', 'The number of virtual machines deployed', 'Network latency between regions'],
  ['A'],
  'Secure score summarises the proportion of recommended security controls that are satisfied, so teams can prioritise improvements and track posture over time.\n\n- **B** is cost management.\n- **C** is inventory.\n- **D** is monitoring.\n\n**Exam tip:** a higher secure score means fewer open recommendations.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(120, 'governance', 'Application monitoring',
  'Which Azure Monitor feature traces requests, dependencies, and exceptions in a web application?',
  ['Application Insights', 'Service Health', 'Cost analysis', 'Azure Policy'],
  ['A'],
  'Application Insights is the application performance monitoring feature of Azure Monitor, with request tracing, dependency maps, live metrics, and failure analysis.\n\n- **B** reports platform incidents.\n- **C** reports cost.\n- **D** enforces configuration.\n\n**Exam tip:** code-level telemetry equals Application Insights.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(121, 'governance', 'Azure Portal',
  'Which statement about the Azure portal is correct?',
  ['It is a web-based graphical interface for managing Azure resources', 'It is a command-line shell', 'It is a database engine', 'It replaces the need for governance'],
  ['A'],
  'The portal is a browser-based console for creating and managing resources, viewing costs, and monitoring health. It is one of several management tools alongside CLI, PowerShell, and SDKs.\n\n- **B** describes Azure CLI or Cloud Shell.\n- **C** is Azure SQL.\n- **D** is false; governance is configured separately.\n\n**Exam tip:** all management tools call the same Azure Resource Manager API.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe management tools'),
Q(122, 'governance', 'Azure CLI',
  'Which tool uses the az command syntax and runs on Windows, Linux, and macOS?',
  ['Azure CLI', 'Azure PowerShell', 'The Azure portal', 'ARM templates'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure CLI is a cross-platform command-line tool whose commands begin with az, for example az group create or az vm list.\n\n- **B** uses PowerShell cmdlets such as New-AzResourceGroup.\n- **C** is graphical.\n- **D** is declarative JSON.\n\n**Exam tip:** az equals CLI; Az equals PowerShell.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe management tools'),
Q(123, 'governance', 'Azure PowerShell',
  'Which command style is used by Azure PowerShell?',
  ['Cmdlets such as New-AzResourceGroup', 'Commands such as az group create', 'YAML manifests only', 'SQL statements'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure PowerShell provides cmdlets in the Az module, using verb-noun naming and PowerShell objects, which suits administrators already invested in PowerShell automation.\n\n- **B** is Azure CLI.\n- **C** is Kubernetes or pipeline configuration.\n- **D** is a database language.\n\n**Exam tip:** match the tool to the team\'s existing scripting skills.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe management tools'),
Q(124, 'governance', 'Azure Cloud Shell',
  'What does Azure Cloud Shell provide?',
  ['A browser-based shell with Azure CLI and PowerShell already authenticated', 'A desktop virtual machine for end users', 'A private network circuit', 'A managed database'],
  ['A'],
  'Cloud Shell is a free, browser-accessible terminal with common tools preinstalled. It authenticates with the signed-in account and persists files in an Azure file share.\n\n- **B** is Azure Virtual Desktop.\n- **C** is ExpressRoute.\n- **D** is Azure SQL.\n\n**Exam tip:** no local install needed - use Cloud Shell.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe management tools'),
Q(125, 'governance', 'ARM templates',
  'What is an Azure Resource Manager template?',
  ['A declarative JSON file that defines the resources to deploy', 'A PowerShell script that creates resources imperatively', 'A container image', 'A monitoring query'],
  ['A'],
  'ARM templates describe the desired end state - resources, properties, and dependencies - and Resource Manager creates or updates them to match. Redeploying the same template is idempotent.\n\n- **B** is imperative automation.\n- **C** is a container artefact.\n- **D** is KQL.\n\n**Exam tip:** declarative means describe the destination, not the steps.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe management tools'),
Q(126, 'governance', 'Bicep',
  'What is Bicep?',
  ['A declarative language that compiles to ARM JSON', 'A container orchestration platform', 'A monitoring query language', 'A relational database engine'],
  ['A'],
  'Bicep is a domain-specific language with cleaner syntax than raw JSON. It transpiles to ARM templates, so the deployment engine and features are the same.\n\n- **B** is AKS.\n- **C** is KQL.\n- **D** is Azure SQL.\n\n**Exam tip:** Bicep is authoring convenience on top of ARM.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe management tools'),
Q(127, 'governance', 'Infrastructure as code',
  'Which two benefits does infrastructure as code provide? (Select two.)',
  ['Repeatable, consistent deployments', 'Version control and review of infrastructure changes', 'Automatic reduction of the Azure bill', 'Removal of the need for access control'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Defining infrastructure in files makes deployments repeatable across environments and lets changes go through source control, review, and history like application code.\n\n- **C** may happen indirectly through right-sizing, but it is not a guaranteed benefit.\n- **D** is false; access control still applies.\n\n**Exam tip:** IaC brings software engineering discipline to infrastructure.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe management tools'),
Q(128, 'governance', 'Deployment choice',
  'A developer needs to script the creation of 50 identical resource groups after hours. Which approach is most appropriate?',
  ['Azure CLI or PowerShell in a script', 'Manual creation in the portal', 'A single resource lock', 'A budget alert'],
  ['A'],
  'Repeatable, unattended work suits command-line scripting with loops, or an ARM template/Bicep deployment, rather than clicking in the portal.\n\n- **B** is slow and error-prone at scale.\n- **C** prevents changes.\n- **D** reports cost.\n\n**Exam tip:** automation beats repetition.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe management tools'),
Q(129, 'governance', 'Azure Trust Center',
  'Where can a customer find Microsoft\'s published compliance offerings and security documentation?',
  ['Microsoft Trust Center', 'Azure Advisor', 'The Azure pricing page', 'Azure Monitor'],
  ['A'],
  'The Trust Center publishes compliance certifications, security practices, and privacy information that organisations use during procurement and audits.\n\n- **B** gives resource recommendations.\n- **C** lists prices.\n- **D** reports telemetry.\n\n**Exam tip:** compliance evidence lives in the Trust Center and Service Trust Portal.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe compliance and privacy'),
Q(130, 'governance', 'Microsoft Privacy Statement',
  'What does the Microsoft Privacy Statement explain?',
  ['What personal data Microsoft collects and how it is used and protected', 'The price of each Azure service', 'How to configure a virtual network', 'Which VM sizes support premium disks'],
  ['A'],
  'The Privacy Statement describes the personal data Microsoft processes, the purposes, and the controls and rights available. It is separate from the contractual terms in the Product Terms and the DPA.\n\n- **B** is pricing.\n- **C** is technical documentation.\n- **D** is product documentation.\n\n**Exam tip:** privacy statement equals personal data handling.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe compliance and privacy'),
Q(131, 'governance', 'Compliance Manager',
  'What is the purpose of Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager?',
  ['Assessing and improving compliance posture against regulations and standards', 'Deploying virtual machines', 'Routing web traffic', 'Storing blobs'],
  ['A'],
  'Compliance Manager scores the tenant against assessments such as ISO 27001 or GDPR, recommends improvement actions, and tracks progress with evidence.\n\n- **B** is compute.\n- **C** is Front Door or Application Gateway.\n- **D** is Blob Storage.\n\n**Exam tip:** Compliance Manager measures your compliance, not Microsoft\'s certifications.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe compliance and privacy'),
Q(132, 'governance', 'Service Level Agreements',
  'What does an Azure service level agreement guarantee?',
  ['A stated level of service availability and the service credits if it is missed', 'That data can never be lost', 'A fixed monthly price', 'That no maintenance will occur'],
  ['A'],
  'SLAs define measurable commitments, most commonly uptime percentages, and describe the credits available if the commitment is missed. They are not a guarantee of zero downtime and do not replace backup design.\n\n- **B** is durability and backup, not an SLA promise.\n- **C** is pricing, not availability.\n- **D** is false; planned maintenance still happens.\n\n**Exam tip:** an SLA is a financial commitment with credits, not a technical guarantee.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe compliance and privacy'),
Q(133, 'governance', 'Preview features',
  'What should a customer expect when using an Azure feature marked as preview?',
  ['It may change or be withdrawn and is not covered by the standard SLA', 'It is fully supported with the same SLA as general availability', 'It costs nothing forever', 'It can never be used in production'],
  ['A'],
  'Preview features are available for evaluation but may change before general availability and are typically excluded from standard SLAs. Many organisations restrict preview use to non-production.\n\n- **B** is false for most previews.\n- **C** is not guaranteed.\n- **D** is a policy choice, not a platform restriction.\n\n**Exam tip:** preview equals evaluate with care.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe compliance and privacy'),
Q(134, 'governance', 'Cost management',
  'Which practice best keeps a development subscription from exceeding its planned spend?',
  ['Set a budget with alerts and review Cost analysis regularly', 'Give every developer the Owner role', 'Disable all resource locks', 'Deploy resources to every region'],
  ['A'],
  'Budgets with alerts provide early warning, and regular Cost analysis shows which resources drive spend so they can be right-sized or removed. Deallocating unused resources has the largest immediate effect.\n\n- **B** increases risk without controlling cost.\n- **C** removes protection.\n- **D** adds spend.\n\n**Exam tip:** visibility plus alerts plus cleanup.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe cost management'),
Q(135, 'governance', 'Governance tools',
  'Which tool should be used to apply the same allowed-locations rule to all subscriptions in a tenant?',
  ['Azure Policy assigned at the tenant root or a management group', 'A resource lock on one resource group', 'An RBAC assignment on each resource', 'A single budget'],
  ['A'],
  'A policy assigned high in the hierarchy inherits to every subscription and resource below it, which enforces the rule without per-resource work.\n\n- **B** protects one scope from deletion.\n- **C** controls access, not location.\n- **D** reports spend.\n\n**Exam tip:** policy inheritance is the mechanism for tenant-wide rules.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(136, 'governance', 'Monitoring',
  'Which data source records control-plane operations such as who deleted a resource?',
  ['The activity log', 'VM guest metrics', 'A storage access tier', 'A DNS zone'],
  ['A'],
  'The activity log records subscription-level control-plane events with the caller, operation, time, and status, which makes it the first place to check for "who changed what".\n\n- **B** is performance telemetry from inside a VM.\n- **C** is a storage setting.\n- **D** is name resolution.\n\n**Exam tip:** activity log equals control-plane audit trail.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(137, 'governance', 'Alerts',
  'What is an action group in Azure Monitor?',
  ['A reusable set of notifications and automated actions triggered by an alert', 'A collection of virtual machines', 'A policy assignment', 'A cost budget'],
  ['A'],
  'An action group defines what happens when an alert fires - email, SMS, voice, push, webhook, Logic App, function, runbook, or ITSM - and can be reused by many alert rules.\n\n- **B** is a scale set or availability set concept.\n- **C** is governance.\n- **D** is cost management.\n\n**Exam tip:** alert rule equals condition; action group equals response.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe monitoring tools'),
Q(138, 'governance', 'Governance design',
  'A company wants separate invoices for development and production but shared policy across both. What should it do?',
  ['Use two subscriptions under one management group', 'Use two resource groups in one subscription', 'Use two regions in one subscription', 'Use two resource locks'],
  ['A'],
  'Subscriptions provide separate billing and access boundaries, while a management group above them applies shared policy and access.\n\n- **B** shares one invoice.\n- **C** does not create a billing boundary.\n- **D** is unrelated.\n\n**Exam tip:** subscriptions split bills; management groups unify governance.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe Azure governance features'),
Q(139, 'governance', 'Cost tools',
  'Which tool estimates the cost savings of moving an existing datacenter to Azure?',
  ['Total Cost of Ownership calculator', 'Azure Pricing calculator', 'Azure Advisor', 'Azure Service Health'],
  ['A'],
  'The TCO calculator compares current on-premises costs with the equivalent Azure costs, producing a migration business case with a break-even view.\n\n- **B** prices a proposed Azure configuration rather than an existing estate.\n- **C** recommends improvements to deployed resources.\n- **D** reports incidents.\n\n**Exam tip:** TCO for migration cases; Pricing calculator for new designs.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe cost management'),
Q(140, 'governance', 'Compliance',
  'Which statement about Microsoft\'s compliance responsibilities is correct?',
  ['Microsoft provides certified services, but the customer must configure and use them compliantly', 'Microsoft guarantees the customer\'s application is compliant', 'Compliance is only the customer\'s responsibility', 'Compliance only matters for government tenants'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft maintains certifications and publishes evidence for its services, while the customer remains responsible for its own data, configuration, access, and business processes. Compliance is shared, much like security.\n\n- **B** is impossible for Microsoft to guarantee.\n- **C** ignores Microsoft\'s certified controls.\n- **D** is false; every regulated industry cares.\n\n**Exam tip:** certified platform plus compliant configuration equals compliance.',
  'Describe Azure management and governance - Describe compliance and privacy')
,
Q(141, 'cloud', 'Case study - cloud model',
  'Contoso wants to stop buying server hardware and pay monthly for the services it uses. Which cloud model best supports this goal?',
  ['Public cloud with consumption-based pricing', 'A private cloud hosted in its own datacenter', 'A fixed-capacity on-premises refresh', 'A perpetual software licence'],
  ['A'],
  'Public cloud removes the upfront hardware purchase and bills monthly for what is consumed, which directly addresses the capital expenditure concern.\n\n- **B** still requires buying and operating hardware.\n- **C** is the current model the company wants to leave.\n- **D** is a licensing pattern that does not provide infrastructure.\n\n**Exam tip:** the case asks for lower CapEx and less infrastructure management - public cloud delivers both.',
  'AZ-900 case study 1', { case: 'contoso-manufacturing', diagram: 'cloud-service-models' }),
Q(142, 'architecture', 'Case study - identity',
  'Contoso staff sign in to Microsoft 365 and on-premises apps with separate accounts. Which Azure service should provide a single cloud identity?',
  ['Microsoft Entra ID', 'Azure DNS', 'Azure Monitor', 'Azure Policy'],
  ['A'],
  'Entra ID is the cloud identity provider for users, groups, and applications. Synchronising the on-premises directory with Entra Connect gives staff one identity for Microsoft 365 and Azure-integrated apps.\n\n- **B** resolves names.\n- **C** monitors telemetry.\n- **D** enforces configuration rules.\n\n**Exam tip:** identity consolidation starts with Entra ID.',
  'AZ-900 case study 1', { case: 'contoso-manufacturing', diagram: 'identity-signin' }),
Q(143, 'cloud', 'Case study - pricing',
  'Which pricing model matches Contoso paying each month for the compute and storage it actually uses?',
  ['Consumption-based pricing', 'A one-off perpetual licence', 'A hardware lease with fixed capacity', 'A flat fee per employee regardless of usage'],
  ['A'],
  'Consumption-based pricing meters usage and bills monthly, so cost follows demand and no upfront capacity purchase is needed.\n\n- **B** is a software licence.\n- **C** is a fixed-capacity lease, closer to the current model.\n- **D** is a per-seat licence, not infrastructure pricing.\n\n**Exam tip:** pay for use equals consumption-based.',
  'AZ-900 case study 1', { case: 'contoso-manufacturing' }),
Q(144, 'architecture', 'Case study - disaster recovery',
  'The factory planning application must survive the loss of an entire Azure region. Which feature should Contoso rely on?',
  ['Azure region pairs with geo-redundant storage and cross-region recovery', 'A single availability zone', 'A larger virtual machine size', 'A resource lock'],
  ['A'],
  'Region pairs place the secondary region at least 300 miles away in the same geography, and geo-redundant storage plus services such as Site Recovery provide the off-site copy and failover the application lacks today.\n\n- **B** protects against a datacenter failure but not a region loss.\n- **C** adds capacity, not resilience.\n- **D** prevents deletion.\n\n**Exam tip:** whole-region loss requires a cross-region design.',
  'AZ-900 case study 1', { case: 'contoso-manufacturing', diagram: 'region-zones-pair' }),
Q(145, 'governance', 'Case study - monitoring',
  'Which service should Contoso administrators use to track the health and performance of the migrated Azure resources?',
  ['Azure Monitor', 'Azure Pricing calculator', 'Azure Policy', 'Azure Resource Manager templates'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure Monitor collects metrics and logs, drives alerts, and feeds dashboards, while Service Health reports platform incidents. Together they cover both resource health and Azure incidents.\n\n- **B** estimates cost.\n- **C** enforces configuration.\n- **D** deploys resources.\n\n**Exam tip:** resource telemetry equals Azure Monitor.',
  'AZ-900 case study 1', { case: 'contoso-manufacturing' }),
Q(146, 'cloud', 'Case study - growth',
  'Fabrikam expects demand to jump from 1,000 to 250,000 users with sharp peaks. Which cloud characteristic supports this?',
  ['Elasticity', 'A fixed-capacity datacenter', 'Perpetual licensing', 'Manual procurement'],
  ['A'],
  'Elasticity automatically adds capacity during demand spikes and removes it afterwards, which suits unpredictable startup growth without over-provisioning.\n\n- **B** and **D** cannot keep pace with the peaks.\n- **C** is a licensing model, not a scaling behaviour.\n\n**Exam tip:** automatic scale out and back in is elasticity.',
  'AZ-900 case study 2', { case: 'fabrikam-growth' }),
Q(147, 'architecture', 'Case study - hosting',
  'Fabrikam has two developers and no operations staff. Which service should host the web front end?',
  ['Azure App Service', 'Azure Virtual Machines', 'Azure ExpressRoute', 'Azure Virtual Desktop'],
  ['A'],
  'App Service is PaaS: it manages the OS, runtime, and scaling so the developers deploy code rather than servers, and it supports continuous deployment from a Git repository.\n\n- **B** leaves patching and scaling to the team.\n- **C** is a private network circuit.\n- **D** delivers desktops to users.\n\n**Exam tip:** minimal administration for a web app equals App Service.',
  'AZ-900 case study 2', { case: 'fabrikam-growth' }),
Q(148, 'architecture', 'Case study - storage',
  'Product photographs are read constantly, change rarely, and must be served cheaply at scale. Which storage choice fits best?',
  ['Blob storage in the hot or cool tier', 'Queue storage', 'Table storage', 'Azure Files'],
  ['A'],
  'Blob storage handles large volumes of unstructured objects such as images and supports access tiers, so frequently read images can sit in hot storage while older ones move to cool.\n\n- **B** holds messages.\n- **C** holds key-value records.\n- **D** provides SMB file shares.\n\n**Exam tip:** images and media equal Blob storage.',
  'AZ-900 case study 2', { case: 'fabrikam-growth', diagram: 'storage-services' }),
Q(149, 'architecture', 'Case study - resilience',
  'Which two design choices help Fabrikam survive the loss of a single datacenter? (Select two.)',
  ['Deploy across availability zones in each region', 'Replicate data and services to a paired region', 'Run everything in one zone to reduce cost', 'Use a single virtual machine for the website'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Zones protect against the loss of one datacenter within a region, and a paired region provides an off-site copy and failover if the whole region is affected. The two layers cover different failure sizes.\n\n- **C** concentrates risk.\n- **D** creates a single point of failure.\n\n**Exam tip:** zones for datacenter loss; pairs for region loss.',
  'AZ-900 case study 2', { case: 'fabrikam-growth', diagram: 'region-zones-pair' }),
Q(150, 'governance', 'Case study - cost planning',
  'Fabrikam must estimate the monthly Azure cost of its planned architecture before launch. Which tool should it use?',
  ['Azure Pricing calculator', 'Azure Advisor', 'Azure Service Health', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud'],
  ['A'],
  'The Pricing calculator lets the team model the planned App Service, storage, database, and bandwidth choices and adjust them before committing, producing a forecast monthly cost.\n\n- **B** reviews resources that already exist.\n- **C** reports platform incidents.\n- **D** reports security posture.\n\n**Exam tip:** before deployment, estimate with the Pricing calculator; after deployment, track with Cost analysis.',
  'AZ-900 case study 2', { case: 'fabrikam-growth' })
);
